A||||1||Cliff Pennock||Fiero Performance?
Z||000000||Cliff Pennock||04-04-99||12:55 AM||||I'm getting mixed feedback about my Fiero's performance. My 86.5 GT Automatic does 0-60mph in 9.2s on a hot day, and 8.2 when it's freezing outside (probably has to do with the denser air). Some say this is quite normal, others say this is way too slow. The engine is stock, no modifications have been made whatsoever.<p>So I would like to settle this once and for all. Is this normal? And if it isn't, what could be the cause that it is so slow (because in my opinion, it is)?  The engine was completely rebuild about 4 years ago.<p>What are your times?
Z||000001||theogre||04-04-99||02:43 AM||||there are a HUGE number of things that affect 0-60 & quarter mile time. You're assumption about air density at lower temp is part of it, and quite correct. Your specific configuration, including: trany/dif gearing, actual CID/Compression (machined engines are ALMOST NEVER stock CID/compression), and half a hundred other things will all change your numbers from someone elses. (let's assume you're testing this at a track for sake of simplicity.)<p>If the car is performing consistantly, there is NOTHING wrong with it.<p>As a matter of comparison...... I used to have a 73 Ford Maveric with a 289cid v8(clean 2bbl carb) and a custom built transmission. It performed almost exactly the same way under the same conditions untill the motor finnaly wore out, but because of it's rear gears however it did 0-60 in about 11 sec. Had I dropped the rear gears to a Ford 9", or changed to a 4bbl carb, I'd probly dropped 2-3 sec off that, if not more. I'd also have lost any resemblance of decent gas milage. (that car got over 22mpg most of the time, and would still tow a barge.) This car would also cruse at over 100mph all day long, something a lower geared rear would have made imposible.<p>In other words..... the people who are telling you your times are wrong generally don't know much. If you asked some one like John Force what they want most out of there car, they would tell you consistant performance is just as, if not more, important than speed. (A good driver can win more races with a slower, more consistant, car, than they will with a fast, iratic, car. This is because they know how the car will perform, and can get a better start. More races are won by good starts than you can imagine.)
